<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 2600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puabi was a queen or noblewoman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> around 2600 BCE, known from her richly furnished tomb in the Royal Cemetery, excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a> in 1927–1928. A cylinder seal names her with the title nin, 'lady' or 'queen'. She was buried with a headdress of gold leaves, jewellery of lapis lazuli and carnelian and dozens of attendants, and her finds are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puabi.jpg" alt="Puabi" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nic McPhee from Morris, Minnesota, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Puabi_with_attendants.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Among about 1,850 burials Woolley counted sixteen or seventeen royal tombs; the unlooted tomb of Queen <b>Puabi</b>, known from a cylinder seal, yielded the Standard of Ur, and the Lyres of Ur, bull-shaped instruments with eleven strings, came to light in 1928.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
